{"name":"Burning Flame","hex":"#ffb162","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"burning","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of burn"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["So hot as to seem to burn (something).","Feeling very hot."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/burning"},{"term":"flame","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible part of fire; a stream of burning vapour or gas, emitting light and heat.","A romantic partner or lover in a usually short-lived but passionate affair."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a brilliant reddish orange-gold colour, like that of a flame."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flame"}],"entries":[{"title":"Burning Flame","blocks":[{"text":"Burning Flame is a TVB modern action drama that focuses on the duties and challenges of Hong Kong firefighters. It is the first installment in the Burning Flame series. The series was specifically filmed to celebrate TVB's 31st Anniversary and was supported by Hong Kong Fire Services Department."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/fe/BurningFlame.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burning_Flame","wikidata":"Q4999846"},{"title":"Burning Flame (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Burning Flame\" is the debut single by English new wave band Vitamin Z, released in 1984. It is from their debut album, Rites of Passage. The song was produced by former Adam and the Ants drummer Chris Hughes, and Ross Cullum. It charted in both the UK and U.S., peaking at numbers 80 and 73, respectively. It also reached No. 27 on the Billboard Dance Club Songs chart. It is the band's best known and most successful song."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burning_Flame_(song)","wikidata":"Q65079938"},{"title":"Combustion","blocks":[{"text":"Combustion, or burning, is a high-temperature exothermic redox chemical reaction between a fuel and an oxidant, usually atmospheric oxygen, that produces oxidized, often gaseous products, in a mixture termed as smoke. Combustion does not always result in fire, because a flame is only visible when substances undergoing combustion vaporize, but when it does, a flame is a characteristic indicator of the reaction. While activation energy must be supplied to initiate combustion, the heat from a flame may provide enough energy to make the reaction self-sustaining. The study of combustion is known as combustion science."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Fire%2C_Fireplace%2C_Rostov-on-Don%2C_Russia.jpg/330px-Fire%2C_Fireplace%2C_Rostov-on-Don%2C_Russia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combustion","wikidata":"Q133235"},{"title":"Flame","blocks":[{"text":"A flame is the visible, gaseous part of a fire. It is caused by a highly exothermic chemical reaction made in a thin zone. When flames are hot enough to have ionized gaseous components of sufficient density, they are then considered plasma."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/DancingFlames.jpg/330px-DancingFlames.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flame","wikidata":"Q235544"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Regenerative_thermal_oxidizer.jpg/500px-Regenerative_thermal_oxidizer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Regenerative_thermal_oxidizer.jpg/1280px-Regenerative_thermal_oxidizer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Regenerative_thermal_oxidizer.jpg/500px-Regenerative_thermal_oxidizer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Air pollution abatement equipment provides combustion control for industrial processes.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARegenerative_thermal_oxidizer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Microgravity_Burning.jpg/500px-Microgravity_Burning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Microgravity_Burning.jpg/1280px-Microgravity_Burning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Microgravity_Burning.jpg/500px-Microgravity_Burning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Colourized gray-scale composite image of the individual frames from a video of a backlit fuel droplet burning in microgravity","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMicrogravity_Burning.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Zoom_lunette_ardente.jpg/500px-Zoom_lunette_ardente.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Zoom_lunette_ardente.jpg/500px-Zoom_lunette_ardente.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Antoine Lavoisier conducting an experiment related to combustion generated by amplified sunlight","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZoom_lunette_ardente.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/500px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/1280px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/500px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spectrum of the blue (premixed, i.e., complete combustion) flame from a butane torch showing molecular radical band emission and Swan bands. Virtually all the light produced is in the blue to green region of the spectrum below about 565 nanometers, accounting for the bluish color of sootless hydrocarbon flames.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/500px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/1280px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/500px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Different flame types of a Bunsen burner depend on oxygen supply. On the left a rich fuel with no premixed oxygen produces a yellow sooty diffusion flame; on the right a lean fully oxygen premixed flame produces no soot and the flame color is produced by molecular radicals, especially CH and C2 band emission.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/1280px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A flame test for sodium. The yellow color in this gas flame does not arise from the black-body emission of soot particles (as the flame is clearly a blue premixed complete combustion flame) but instead comes from the spectral line emission of sodium atoms, specifically the very intense sodium D lines.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlametest--Na.swn.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Candlespace.jpg/500px-Candlespace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Candlespace.jpg/500px-Candlespace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In zero-G, convection does not carry the hot combustion products away from the fuel source, resulting in a spherical flame front.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACandlespace.jpg"}],"sounds":{"burning":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/En-us-burning.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/4/41/En-us-burning.ogg/En-us-burning.ogg.mp3"}],"flame":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/98/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-flame.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/98/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-flame.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-flame.wav